WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Files can look completely different on the
outside. A PDF from Gmail, an image from a

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:12.080
generation, an audio clip, a document from
a parser. But for workflows to use them

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.380
reliably, they need a common shape. This
is the file object. It gives Sim one

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.120
consistent interface to pass files between
blocks so producers and consumers can work

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.840
together without every file source needing
special handling. Let me show you how the

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.520
files object works. Your workflows make
files from very different places. An email

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.980
arrives carrying an attachment. One block
generates an image, another turns text

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:45.800
into speech, and another reads a document.
Wire them all into one agent, and every

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.140
lane feeds the same reader. The Gmail
trigger fires first, and its attachment

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.580
lands in the workflow as a file object.
Then the image, the audio clip, and the

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.660
parsed document arrive, each one delivered
exactly the same way.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.040
Look at what's settled underneath. Four
very different payloads, and the identical

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.920
package around every one of them. Run it
at full speed, and whatever a lane

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.720
produces, the agent simply reads the next
file object.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.760
Catch one mid -run and look closer.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:26.180
Every file object carries the same fields,
an ID, a name, a URL, where the bytes

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.560
live, a size, a type, and the content
itself. A couple more fields exist under

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.600
the hood, but this shape never changes, no
matter which block made the file. Put it

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.280
back, and the machine keeps moving.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.420
Because every producer speaks the same
format, you can swap which one feeds the

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.680
agent, and the agent side never changes.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.840
It works downstream, too. The same object
can ride to any consumer, into an agent,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.920
out as an email attachment, or straight
into storage. This is a real template. An

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.740
invoice lands in Gmail, gets parsed, an
agent pulls out the fields, and the result

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.680
settles in your database. One object
riding the whole way through.
